WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.290
Film Courage: Can you talk us through how
you make a beat sheet?

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:08.830
Larry Wilson, screenwriter BEETLEJUICE, TALES
FROM THE CRYPT, THE ADDAMS FAMILY: I don’t.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:14.099
I don’t. I will if contractually obliged
doing a beat sheet or an outline I will do

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:19.900
it. And sometimes you are and what usually
happens is you spend all this time on this

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.689
beat sheet and this outline and it’s the
most bastardized thing in the world because

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:29.769
it’s not a script, it’s not a story, it’s
this thing that exists halfway between a script

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:36.001
and a story. But I will do it if contractually
obliged to, I’ll do an outline and I’ll

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:40.200
do a very detailed outline and I’ll do it
always to the best of my abilities, I won’t

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.950
slum but I’ll do it and hand it in and the
producers will go “We love this outline!

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:48.559
This is a great outline. Now go write the
script.”

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:51.820
And you’ll go write the script and you’ll
have your outline and you’ll say “Okay,

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.970
here’s what the outline is telling me and
you start writing and it will immediately

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:02.300
start changing and better ideas will emerge
and the next thing you know the outline is

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.450
completely out the window and you’ve written
a script and you’ll turn it in and the producers

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.800
will go “We love this! This is just like
the outline.” And everyone has forgotten

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:20.570
the outline and I know again, my beat sheet
is obviously you kind of know the beginning.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:24.230
You know you’re putting your characters
and you feel these characters and you put

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:30.040
them in this situation that they are going
to have to fight for their lives to get out

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.540
of.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.979
I kind of know what the end is going to be
(kind of sort of) and then it’s the great

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:44.259
unknown and to do that in a beat sheet to
me it’s just taking all the fun out of it.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:51.020
So don’t listen to me, listen to all the
other people out there who say outline, beat

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:55.140
sheets. Please if that’s what you need to
do and there’s great writers who do that,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:00.939
of course they do that. And sometimes when
you’re collaborating it becomes more necessary

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:09.760
because you’re sending stuff. But for me,
they don’t work for me and any time I do

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.940
them everything starts changing anyways so
much that they quickly become irrelevant.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.850
Film Courage: Why don’t they work for you?

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:28.670
Larry: Because I’m a storyteller and the
stories emerge from some place that is beat

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:36.280
sheet-proof. That’s just me. I don’t know
what…they just come from somewhere else

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:43.120
and if I tell it all in a quote-unquote “beat
sheet” it’s like why would I do that to

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:49.310
myself. Why would I take all the fun out of
discovering it and putting it in this thing

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:54.460
that’s just kind of bullet points.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:03:03.090
But that’s me and that’s the idiosyncratic
part of me but I’m in good company Stephen

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:17.790
King, my favorite writer, ask him about beat
sheets, help me with the writer who wrote

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:23.000
Game of Thrones (voice from off-camera “George
R. R. Martin”) Thank you! He said it the

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.440
best of anyone he said there are…now you
would think particularly if you’ve read

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:37.910
the Game of Thrones books that those things
are outlined completely. They are so intricate

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:44.410
they are so full of plot and character and
this needs to overlap and this needs to…you

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:51.700
know what I mean, that they are extremely
intricate stories. He doesn’t outline and

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.250
what he says about it is and I think it’s
such a great quote and I’m paraphrasing

00:03:55.250 --> 00:04:01.031
a little bit but the essence of it is there
are architects and there are gardeners. An

00:04:01.031 --> 00:04:08.010
architect will need that blueprint to build
what it is they are going to build. He said

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:16.870
I’m a gardener. I plant a seed and I tend
it and nurse it and watch it grow. And there

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:22.150
are architects who will do that beat sheet
and will do it brilliantly and so I’m not

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:28.380
knocking it and then there are gardeners and
I’m a gardener. I just kind of let the idea

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.320
sit there and I tend it and I take good care
of it and I make sure it gets all of the water

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.979
and Sun and I watch it grow.

